WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.174
Borrowing allows code to use a value
without taking ownership.

2
00:00:03.254 --> 00:00:05.221
A shared reference permits read-only
access.

3
00:00:05.301 --> 00:00:07.672
A mutable reference permits controlled
modification.

4
00:00:07.752 --> 00:00:09.669
In both cases, the borrower receives
access,

5
00:00:09.749 --> 00:00:13.230
but the original owner remains responsible
for the value's lifetime and cleanup.

6
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:16.842
Lifetimes describe how long references are
valid relative to the data they borrow.

7
00:00:16.922 --> 00:00:19.091
A reference must not outlive the owner of
that data.

8
00:00:19.171 --> 00:00:22.803
This is one of the core reasons safe Rust
eliminates ordinary use-after-free errors:

9
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:25.557
the compiler checks the relationship
before the program runs.

10
00:00:25.637 --> 00:00:27.403
Borrow checker messages can feel strict,

11
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.703
but they often identify a real lifecycle
ambiguity.

12
00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:32.910
A function may be trying to return a
reference to data that will disappear.

13
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.210
A task may be trying to hold a borrowed
value too long.

14
00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:38.670
A scope may be mixing mutation and shared
access in a way that obscures who can use

15
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:39.204
the value.

16
00:00:39.284 --> 00:00:41.252
Returning references requires special
care.

17
00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:44.460
A returned reference must point to data
that remains valid for the caller.

18
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.053
If the reference is tied to an input,

19
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.756
the function signature should make that
relationship clear.

20
00:00:48.836 --> 00:00:51.258
If no valid owner will remain after the
function returns,

21
00:00:51.338 --> 00:00:53.356
the function should return owned data
instead.

22
00:00:53.436 --> 00:00:56.211
In security review, ask what owns the data
behind each reference.

23
00:00:56.291 --> 00:00:58.107
Ask whether that owner outlives every use.

24
00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:01.466
Ask whether a reference crosses async,
thread, callback, or FFI boundaries.

25
00:01:01.546 --> 00:01:04.876
Good lifetime design makes those answers
visible instead of relying on tribal

26
00:01:04.956 --> 00:01:05.460
knowledge.
